DAY 15 — CROSS-SITE SCRIPTING (XSS)

XSS = Cross-Site Scripting:
XSS occurs when untrusted user-controlled input reaches a webpage/browser context in an unsafe way and can be interpreted as executable code.

CORE DIFFERENCE:
SQLi → Targets database/query logic
XSS  → Targets browser/webpage behavior

BASIC FLOW:
User Input → Application → HTML/JS/DOM → Browser

TYPES OF XSS:

1.Reflected XSS Input is sent in a request and reflected back in the server response.

Flow: Input → Request → Server → Response → Browser

2.Stored XSS Input is stored by the application/database and later displayed to users.

Flow: Input → Storage → Webpage → Browser

Stored XSS can potentially affect multiple users.

3.DOM-based XSS Client-side JavaScript processes attacker-controlled data unsafely and places it into a dangerous DOM/browser context.

Flow: Input → JavaScript → DOM → Browser

SOURCE:
The location where user-controlled data enters the application.

Examples:
URL
Query parameter
Form input
Cookie
Request body

SINK:
A browser/API operation where data is inserted or interpreted in a potentially dangerous context.

Examples can involve:
HTML
Attributes
JavaScript
DOM manipulation

XSS TESTING WORKFLOW:
Find input ↓ Send harmless test value ↓ Find where the value appears ↓ Identify the context ↓ Determine how the application handles it ↓ Test only within an authorized lab

IMPORTANT:
Not every reflected input is XSS.
The important question is:
"Can user-controlled data be interpreted as executable code in the browser?"

MAIN DEFENSES:
Context-aware output encoding
Safe HTML/DOM APIs
Input validation where appropriate
Content Security Policy (CSP) as defense-in-depth

KEY MEMORY:
Reflected → Request → Response → Browser Stored    → Storage → Page → Browser DOM       → JavaScript/DOM → Browser

CORE TAKEAWAY:
XSS is fundamentally about untrusted data reaching a browser context where it can be interpreted as executable code.
